            body: 'Take the numbers above: $600,000 funded, $450,000 spent, so $150,000 of funded balance remains. If the ETC to finish the current scope is $210,000, a $60,000 shortfall is coming. This exact scenario is why FAR 52.232-22 (Limitation of Funds) and FAR 52.232-20 (Limitation of Cost) exist, and it is one of the most important clauses you will ever apply as a PM. On an incrementally funded cost-reimbursement contract, the contractor must notify the government in writing once they project hitting a set percentage of the funded amount, commonly 75%, with enough lead time for the government to act. From there, the government either processes a funding modification to add more money, or the contractor stops work at the funded ceiling. A contractor is not obligated to keep working past their funded amount, and a government PM who pressures them to do so anyway is creating an Anti-Deficiency Act problem, not just an awkward conversation.',
