            body: 'On the same $10M contract, an Agent booking a 5% fee reports $500K of revenue at a 100% gross margin. A Principal reports $10M of revenue at, say, a 12% gross margin. Profit in dollars can be similar. Everything else is different. Revenue is twenty times larger, which changes the company\'s size category with banks, buyers, and customers. Gross margin percentage drops sharply, which finance must explain to anyone comparing the two years. Receivables and unbilled balances grow, because the whole contract value now flows through the books, which changes working capital needs. Loan covenants based on revenue or leverage ratios move. And the company now needs the processes to actually control the work it is now reporting: subcontract management, quality acceptance, technical direction. Moving from Agent to Principal is not a paperwork change. It is a change in what the company does.',
