        title: 'IAC vs. MAC vs. GWAC: Sorting Out the Alphabet',
        body: 'These three terms get used interchangeably in conversation, but they\'re not the same thing. Think of them as nested categories.\n\nAn IAC (Interagency Acquisition Contract) is the broadest term. Any contract vehicle established by one agency for use by others. GWACs are a subset of IACs.\n\nA MAC (Multiple Award Contract) describes the award structure: multiple vendors win simultaneously, each holding a "seat" on the vehicle. When the government has work, it competes a task order among those awardees. A MAC can be agency-specific (only one agency uses it) or government-wide.\n\nA GWAC (Government-Wide Acquisition Contract) is a MAC that has been designated for use by any federal agency. GSA administers the major GWACs. OASIS+, Alliant 2, STARS III. OMB policy puts pressure on agencies to use existing GWACs rather than creating duplicative vehicles. Before an agency can set up its own competing contract, they\'re supposed to justify why a GWAC doesn\'t meet their needs.',
