        body: 'Treating TDL management as a disciplined process, not just reading emails and starting work, is what separates experienced GSA vehicle PMs from the ones who end up in disputes.\n\nStep 1: Read it carefully. Is the directed work within the scope of your task order SOW? Not just vaguely related to the mission. Actually within the authorized scope language.\n\nStep 2: Check the CLIN. Is there funded ceiling available under the right CLIN to cover this work? If the TDL directs work under a CLIN that\'s nearly exhausted, flag it immediately before starting.\n\nStep 3: If it\'s within scope and funded, acknowledge receipt in writing. A simple email back to the COR works. "Received TDL-2026-007 dated March 31. We will commence the directed activities on April 7 as specified."\n\nStep 4: Execute and document. Keep records of what you did in response to the TDL, when, by whom, and at what cost. When you invoice, reference the TDL number.\n\nStep 5: If it\'s not within scope, or if you\'re not sure. Stop and write to the CO. Not the COR. The CO. Clearly, professionally, in writing. "We received TDL-2026-008 directing support for the cloud migration effort. We want to flag that cloud migration services do not appear to be within the current SOW. Please advise whether a task order modification is needed before we proceed."',
