            type: 'text',
            heading: 'Over Time vs. Point in Time: The Test That Decides Your Revenue Pattern',
            body: 'Point in time means you record revenue when the customer takes control of the deliverable. Deliver 500 laptops, the government accepts them, you book the revenue that day. Simple.\n\nOver time means you record revenue progressively while you do the work. ASC 606 allows this when any one of three conditions is true: the customer receives and consumes the benefit as you perform (typical for services), your work creates or enhances an asset the customer controls (typical for work on government-furnished equipment or a government facility), or your work creates an asset with no alternative use to you and you have an enforceable right to payment for work completed to date.\n\nThat third condition is why most defense development and production contracts book revenue over time. A radar built to a military specification has no alternative buyer, and the termination for convenience clause guarantees the contractor is paid for work performed plus a reasonable profit if the government cancels. No alternative use, enforceable right to payment. Over time it is.',
