        quiz: [
          { id: 'q1', question: 'Under the FAR limitations on pass-through charges, what triggers the requirement to disclose and describe added value in a proposal?', options: ['Any use of subcontractors', 'Intending to subcontract more than 70% of the total cost of the work', 'Subcontracting to a foreign company', 'A contract value over $100M'], correct: 1, explanation: 'The 70% threshold is the trigger. Above it, the offeror must identify the subcontracted amount and percentage and describe the value it adds.' },
          { id: 'q2', question: 'If the contracting officer determines a prime adds no or negligible value to subcontracted work, what happens?', options: ['The contract is terminated', 'The subcontractor is paid directly by the government', 'The prime\'s indirect costs and profit on that subcontracted work become unallowable', 'The prime must reduce its subcontracting to 50%'], correct: 2, explanation: 'Excessive pass-through charges, meaning the markup applied to work the prime does not meaningfully manage, are unallowable. The prime still pays its subs but does not recover the markup.' },
          { id: 'q3', question: 'Which of these is NOT one of the FAR\'s examples of added value?', options: ['Performing quality assurance functions', 'Coordinating deliveries', 'Holding the prime contract', 'Managing multiple sources for contract requirements'], correct: 2, explanation: 'Holding the contract is not a subcontract management function. The examples are activities such as QA, coordinating deliveries, managing sources, maintaining inventory, and reducing lead times.' },
          { id: 'q4', question: 'Company A subcontracts $25M of a $30M contract and forwards invoices with a markup. An auditor is most likely to classify Company A as:', options: ['A Principal booking $30M', 'An Agent booking only its fee', 'A joint venture', 'A pass-through entity booking $25M'], correct: 1, explanation: 'Without control of the service (direction, responsibility, risk, pricing), the company is an Agent and recognizes only its fee or margin as revenue.' },
          { id: 'q5', question: 'What is the strongest practical evidence that a prime adds value to subcontracted work?', options: ['A paragraph in the proposal describing oversight', 'Written technical direction, QA and acceptance records, and integration test results performed by the prime\'s staff', 'A large G&A rate', 'The subcontractor\'s own quality certifications'], correct: 1, explanation: 'Added value is proven by what the prime actually does. Records of direction, inspection, acceptance, and integration are the evidence the CO and DCAA look for.' },
          { id: 'q6', question: 'Why do some contractors use a separate subcontract handling rate instead of full G&A on subcontract costs?', options: ['The FAR requires it', 'To price sub-heavy work more competitively and tie the markup to the actual cost of managing subs', 'To avoid DCAA audits', 'To increase profit on subcontracts'], correct: 1, explanation: 'A handling rate built from the cost of procurement, subcontract administration, and QA is both more competitive and easier to defend as added value than a full G&A load.' },
          { id: 'q7', question: 'A small business prime on a services set-aside subcontracts 75% of the work to a large business. Which rule has it violated outright?', options: ['ASC 606', 'The limitations on subcontracting rule for set-asides', 'The 70% pass-through disclosure rule', 'CAS'], correct: 1, explanation: 'Set-aside primes must generally perform at least 50% of the amount paid for services themselves (or with similarly situated small subs). The 70% rule requires disclosure; the limitations on subcontracting rule is a hard ceiling.' },
          { id: 'q8', question: 'A large prime subcontracts 80% of a full-and-open contract but runs the system design, integration lab, and acceptance testing with its own engineers. Which statement is correct?', options: ['It violates the pass-through rule because it exceeds 70%', 'It must disclose under the 70% rule, and its documented added value should make the markup allowable and support Principal accounting', 'It is automatically an Agent', 'It cannot charge any profit on subcontracts'], correct: 1, explanation: 'The 70% threshold triggers disclosure, not a prohibition. Real, documented integration and acceptance work demonstrates added value and control.' },
          { id: 'q9', question: 'During due diligence, how do acquirers typically treat pass-through revenue?', options: ['At the same multiple as self-performed revenue', 'They strip it out or value it at a much lower multiple', 'They ignore it', 'They add a premium for it'], correct: 1, explanation: 'Pass-through revenue carries thin margin, replacement risk, and allowability exposure, so buyers value it far below self-performed revenue.' },
          { id: 'q10', type: 'drag_match', question: 'Match the rule to the question it asks:', options: [], correct: 0, explanation: 'The pass-through rule asks about added value above 70% subcontracting. Limitations on subcontracting asks whether a set-aside prime performs enough itself. ASC 606 asks whether the company controls the service.', pairs: [ { left: 'FAR limitations on pass-through charges', right: 'Does the prime add value when subcontracting over 70%?' }, { left: 'Limitations on subcontracting (set-asides)', right: 'Does the small business prime perform at least its required share?' }, { left: 'ASC 606 Principal vs. Agent', right: 'Does the company control the service before it transfers?' } ] },
          { id: 'q11', type: 'drag_order', question: 'Order the steps for converting a pass-through program into a value-add program:', options: [], correct: 0, explanation: 'Map who does what, move acceptance and quality in-house, issue written technical direction, own integration and risk, then fix the SOW and rates on the next contract action once the work is real.', orderedItems: ['Map who actually performs each subcontracted function', 'Move acceptance and quality in-house', 'Issue technical direction to subs in writing', 'Own system integration and warranty', 'Update the SOW and handling rates on the next action'] },
          { id: 'q12', question: 'A proposal describes an "integration lab" and "supplier quality audits" that the company does not actually perform. Beyond failing the added-value test, what is the main risk?', options: ['Losing small business status', 'A potential false claims problem, because the government is billed for value not delivered', 'An automatic EVM finding', 'Nothing, as long as the subs perform well'], correct: 1, explanation: 'Charging for management functions that are not performed is misrepresentation, not just an allowability issue. Do the work first, then describe it.' },
        ],
