            body: 'The mechanism is simple and vicious. Low utilization pushes labor into overhead. Higher overhead raises the fully burdened rate for everyone. Higher rates make proposals less competitive. Fewer wins mean more people on the bench. Lower utilization. Senior leaders at services companies recognize this spiral early because they watch two numbers weekly: company-wide utilization and the gap between actual indirect rates and the provisional rates being billed.\n\nBreaking it requires unpopular decisions: releasing bench staff quickly rather than hoping for a task order, freezing hiring until funding is signed, moving indirect work off the overhead pool where legitimate, and in some cases restructuring into separate cost pools so one struggling business unit does not raise rates for the whole company. PMs are usually the first to see the spiral start, because they are the ones holding people through the gap.',
