            body: 'On cost-reimbursement contracts the company bills actual cost at provisional indirect rates plus fee. If utilization drops and the overhead rate rises, the higher rate gets billed, so the immediate margin damage is smaller than on fixed-rate work. That is why cost-type portfolios feel safer to services executives.\n\nTwo things keep the discipline in place anyway. First, provisional rates are approved estimates; if actuals run well above them, the company either gets a rate adjustment (with scrutiny) or eats the difference at final settlement. Second, higher rates on cost-type work still make the company less competitive on the next proposal, because evaluators compare proposed rates across bidders. A cost-type contract cushions a utilization problem for a year. It does not solve it.',
