            body: 'The Business module described the set-aside cliff. Affiliation is the mechanism. When a large company buys a small one, the small one is affiliated with its new parent from the closing date, and its size includes the parent\'s. Recertification is required within 30 days, and the company can no longer certify as small on new offers. The same happens with a minority investment that carries negative control, a management agreement that hands operations to a large partner, or a "strategic alliance" that makes the small firm economically dependent. Senior leaders on both sides of a transaction model the affiliation consequences before signing, because a deal structured to "keep the company small" that actually creates affiliation exposes both parties to false certification claims.',
