            body: 'DCAA conducts post-award audits on certified contracts, often two to four years later, comparing the data the contractor possessed at certification against what it submitted. When it finds undisclosed data that would have reduced the price, the government is entitled to a price reduction equal to the overstatement plus the profit or fee on it, with interest from the date of overpayment. If the government shows the defective pricing was knowing, penalties equal to the overpayment are added. The contractor\'s defenses are narrow: that the government knew the data anyway, that the data did not affect the price, or that the government did not rely on it. "We did not think it was significant" is not a defense; significance is judged by whether a prudent buyer would have wanted to know.',
