            body: 'Organizational conflicts of interest are covered in the Section H lesson, but they intersect with personal ethics constantly. A contractor providing advisory services to a program office sees requirements before they are solicited; that is unequal access, an OCI, and also creates a PIA exposure if the information is proposal-related. An employee who helped write a statement of work for the government cannot help their new employer respond to it. Mitigation plans (firewalls, recusals, information barriers) address the organizational conflict, but the individual restrictions still apply to the people involved. Companies that pursue both advisory and product work on the same programs need both OCI mitigation and personal ethics controls, and they need to be able to prove both were followed.',
