            body: 'Proposals that cluster far from the IGCE tell the government something. All of them well above: the requirement is bigger than the estimator thought, the rates are stale, or industry is pricing risk the government did not see; the estimate should be re-examined before concluding the market is overpriced. All well below: the requirement is ambiguous enough that industry is bidding a narrower reading, or the estimate over-scoped the work. One far below the rest: a possible buy-in or misunderstanding, which cost realism (on cost-type) or price realism (on fixed-price, if the solicitation provided for it) should test. Senior PMs treat the spread between proposals and the IGCE as information about the requirement, not just about the bidders.',
