            body: 'A factor earns its place if it will separate offerors on something the government cares about and can evaluate. The common failure is factor lists that evaluate everything: technical approach, management approach, staffing, transition, quality control, risk, past performance, small business, price, each with subfactors, each with page limits. Every competent offeror scores Acceptable on most of them, the evaluators spend weeks documenting non-differences, and the decision comes down to one or two things that could have been the whole evaluation. The discipline is to ask, for each candidate factor: will credible offerors differ on this, does the difference matter to performance, and can evaluators tell the difference from a proposal. Three to five factors that discriminate beat ten that do not. Past performance and price are nearly always among them; the technical factors should be the two or three things that actually drive success on this requirement.',
