            body: 'In uniform, your rank told people how to read you before you spoke. In a civilian interview, nothing does. Interviewers are asking three things: can you do the job, will you fit, and do you want it. Answer with specific stories in the STAR shape (situation, task, action, result) using translated language and civilian units. Avoid the passive voice of military writing ("the mission was accomplished"); say what you did. Do not lead with hardship or sacrifice; lead with what you delivered. Ask about the program, the customer, and the team, because those questions show you understand what the job is. And be ready for "why are you leaving the military," with an answer about what you are moving toward rather than what you are leaving.',
