            body: 'The Armed Services Procurement Act took the wartime authorities and made them permanent, but with a philosophy: the normal way to buy would be formal advertising (publish the requirement, receive sealed bids, award to the lowest responsive and responsible bidder), and negotiation would be allowed only under seventeen enumerated exceptions. The exceptions turned out to cover most of what the military bought (anything technical, anything urgent, anything for research), so in practice negotiated procurement dominated defense buying from the start. But the law\'s structure made negotiation look like the exception, which meant every negotiated award carried a justification, and that habit of documenting why competition was not used survived into the Competition in Contracting Act four decades later. The lesson on J&As is the modern descendant.',
