            body: 'Before CICA, GAO decided protests as a matter of its own practice, agencies could ignore it, and there was no stay; a protester could win and find the contract already performed. CICA gave GAO statutory authority, set deadlines (a protest filed within a defined window after award or debriefing triggers a stay of performance; pre-award protests stay award), required agencies to report, and gave GAO a deadline to decide. Agencies may override the stay with a written finding of urgent and compelling circumstances or best interests, and those findings are themselves reviewable. The Court of Federal Claims offers a parallel forum. The practical result: a losing bidder with a credible ground has real leverage, agencies write evaluations to survive review, and the source selection lesson\'s emphasis on evaluating exactly as the solicitation stated is the direct product of forty years of GAO decisions sustaining protests when they did not.',
