            type: 'text',
            heading: 'The Commission on Wartime Contracting',
            body: 'Congress created a commission in 2008 to examine wartime contracting in Iraq and Afghanistan. Its final report in 2011 estimated that of roughly $200 billion in contract spending, somewhere between $31 and $60 billion had been lost to waste and fraud. Its findings were institutional rather than prosecutorial: the government deployed without enough contracting officers and oversight personnel; it used cost-type contracts without the oversight those contracts require; it built facilities and programs without sustainment plans, so that finished projects were abandoned; it let competition lapse in favor of speed for years at a time; and it had no doctrine for contractors as part of the force. The recommendations shaped subsequent law: more contingency contracting personnel, a deployable contracting capability, planning for sustainment before construction, and clearer rules on what contractors may do.',
