            heading: 'Why Services Were Harder Than Products',
            body: 'The acquisition system was built to buy things: a requirement, a design, a test, a production run, a fielded item with a sustainment tail. Services fit none of it. There is no milestone structure, no design review, no physical acceptance. Requirements are written as levels of effort or as performance standards that are hard to measure. Quality is judged by whether the customer is satisfied, which is not a metric. And the same contract can be renewed for a decade with nobody asking whether the need still exists, because there is no production decision that forces the question. The preaward module\'s services lesson describes the fixes that emerged: performance work statements with measurable standards, quality assurance surveillance plans, SRRBs to validate that a service is still needed, and tradecraft guidance for services acquisition. They are recent, and they exist because services outgrew the system that was supposed to govern them.',
