            body: 'Three things carry forward. First, services contracts are the bulk of the market, they are recompeted every few years, and they are won and lost on price and past performance rather than technology, which the capture module explains. Second, the oversight apparatus built after the wars is now standard: CORs with certifications, QASPs with measurable standards, surveillance, and CPARS ratings that follow the company. A contractor that treats the QASP as the definition of the job rather than as paperwork tends to keep the contract. Third, the inherently governmental line is enforced unevenly, and a contractor employee who is quietly making government decisions is exposed even though the government asked them to. The professional move is to write the recommendation, name the decision maker, and get the signature.',
